  "rationale": "**Verdict: the requirement is met.** The code implements each clause, and the tests cover them.\n\n**Implementation (`src/main.rs`)**\n- The CLI is a clap `Parser` with a `Subcommand` enum. It has no `agents` command, and `Setup` accepts only `--path`. Clap therefore rejects `habeas agents`, `habeas unknown`, `habeas setup --install-skills` and `habeas setup --wrong` with a nonzero exit and a \"Usage: habeas …\" message on stderr.\n- `MdbookCommand::Supports { renderer }` is handled in `main`. It returns `Ok(())` when the renderer is `html` and calls `std::process::exit(1)` otherwise.\n- `main` skips workspace evidence loading for `mdbook supports`. The support query therefore does not depend on the working directory's configuration.\n- `Cli::parse()` runs before anything else, so parse errors surface as usage failures.\n\n**Tests (`tests/cli.rs`)**\n- `renderer_support_and_invalid_setup_option` checks the following:\n  - `mdbook supports html` succeeds.\n  - `mdbook supports epub` fails.\n  - `agents`, `setup --wrong`, `setup --install-skills` and `setup --path . --install-skills` each fail and print \"Usage: habeas\" on stderr.\n- `unknown_command_fails_with_usage` runs `habeas unknown`. It asserts a nonzero exit and \"Usage: habeas\" in stderr. It carries `{test}` references to both `requirements.cli.invocation` and `reference.cli.unknown_command`.\n\n**Example `reference.cli.unknown_command`** (`md/reference.md`:22): `unknown_command_fails_with_usage` covers its scenario, a nonzero exit plus a usage message.\n\n**Gaps:** I found none. The invalid setup option combinations in the requirement are covered, including the explicit `--path . --install-skills` case.",
